            <ArticleTitle>[Soluble adhesion molecules (P-selectin, ICAM-1 and ICAM-3) in rheumatoid arthritis].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>17-20</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Investigation of serum levels and clinical role of soluble intercellular molecules of adhesion (pICAM-1, PICAM-3 and pP-selectin) in rheumatoid arthritis (RA).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIALS AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Enzyme immunoassay with Bender MedSystem kits (Austria) was employed to measure serum concentration of soluble intercellular molecules of adhesion in 36 RA patients.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Elevated levels of serum pICAM-1, pICAM-3 and pP-selectin were registered in 74.2, 28.6 and 25.7% of RA patients, respectively. Content of pP-selectin more strongly correlated with activity and severity indices than that of pICAM-3 (p &lt; 0.001). Content of pICAM-1 and clinical picture of RA were unrelated.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Levels of pP-selectine can characterize RA activity.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
